findings.body:
The guard now checks `dep.name` instead of `full_name`:

::diff{file="src/prefect/_sdk/fetcher.py" lines="395-418"}

Take `--deployment production`. For any deployment called `production`, `dep.name in deployment_names` is true, so the whole block is skipped. That block is the only place `short_name_matches` gets filled, so the warning "Short deployment name 'production' matched N deployments across different flows" (lines 446-452) is never produced.

`test_fetch_sdk_data_ambiguous_short_name_warning` in `tests/_sdk/test_fetcher.py` (around line 597) covers exactly this case. I couldn't run the suite here (the environment is broken), but reading the code, that test will fail.

The comment just above the guard still says "check the full name matches", which no longer matches the code. The original check was right: skip the loop only when the full name matches exactly, and go through it otherwise so short-name matches get recorded.

::suggestion{file="src/prefect/_sdk/fetcher.py" lines="397-397"}
        if deployment_names and full_name not in deployment_names:

The `isinstance(variables, dict)` check is gone. Now any value stored under `base_job_template['variables']` goes straight into `WorkPoolInfo.job_variables_schema`:

::diff{file="src/prefect/_sdk/fetcher.py" lines="178-188"}

`renderer.py` (around line 271) then runs `schema.get("properties")`. If a work pool has a malformed template where `variables` is a list, string, or other non-dict, this raises `AttributeError` and the whole `prefect sdk generate` run fails. With the guard, that pool would just have produced no `with_infra()`.

The field is typed `dict[str, Any]`, so the guard is what made that type true. Templates come from the server and can be edited by users, so the code shouldn't trust their shape.

::suggestion{file="src/prefect/_sdk/fetcher.py" lines="181-182"}
        if base_job_template and "variables" in base_job_template:
            variables = base_job_template["variables"]
            if isinstance(variables, dict):
                job_vars_schema = variables

`run_async()` returns a `PrefectFlowRunFuture`. On that class, `result()` is a normal sync method that wraps `aresult()` with `run_coro_as_sync` (`src/prefect/futures.py` ~410-417). It blocks and returns the plain value, so `await future.result()` raises `TypeError: object ... can't be used in 'await' expression`, unless the flow happens to return something awaitable.

::diff{file="docs/v3/advanced/generate-custom-sdk.mdx" lines="124-136"}

